WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.290 --> 00:00:05.859
Whenever an actor decides to use "the method"
approach to a role, it usually ends up making

00:00:05.859 --> 00:00:06.859
headlines.

00:00:06.859 --> 00:00:10.650
The process of an actor really getting their
head inside a character has made for some

00:00:10.650 --> 00:00:14.889
jaw-dropping performances, and some shocking
behind-the-scenes tales.

00:00:14.889 --> 00:00:20.090
Here are just a few times method actors have
taken things too far for a part.

00:00:20.090 --> 00:00:21.090
Adrien Brody

00:00:21.090 --> 00:00:26.910
In 2002, Adrien Brody scored the role of a
lifetime when director Roman Polanski picked

00:00:26.910 --> 00:00:32.800
him to star as Polish-Jewish musician and
composer Władysław Szpilman in The Pianist.

00:00:32.800 --> 00:00:38.800
The actor immediately buried himself in Szpilman's
World War II memoirs in order to better understand

00:00:38.800 --> 00:00:40.030
the role.

00:00:40.030 --> 00:00:44.830
After being forced into the Warsaw Ghetto
and separated from his family, Szpilman eluded

00:00:44.830 --> 00:00:49.400
Nazi forces and survived the Holocaust by
hiding in the ruins of Warsaw.

00:00:49.400 --> 00:00:54.200
To prepare for the part, the six-foot-one
Brody spent six weeks on an intense diet to

00:00:54.200 --> 00:00:58.510
get down to a mere 130 pounds before filming
began.

00:00:58.510 --> 00:01:02.810
For the better part of a year, Brody essentially
"dropped out" of modern society.

00:01:02.810 --> 00:01:06.100
He moved to Europe, sold his car, and disconnected
his cellphone.

00:01:06.100 --> 00:01:12.360
"Because the character loses so much, I wanted
to kind of have a glimpse of what that loss

00:01:12.360 --> 00:01:13.530
would feel like."

00:01:13.530 --> 00:01:17.360
After filming wrapped, it took over half a
year for Brody to recover.

00:01:17.360 --> 00:01:18.549
He later admitted:

00:01:18.549 --> 00:01:23.290
"There were times when I was concerned that
I might not be able to get out of it sane,

00:01:23.290 --> 00:01:26.549
because I didn't realize how far it had taken
me."

00:01:26.549 --> 00:01:31.299
In the end, his dedication to the role paid
off in a big way, not only did The Pianist

00:01:31.299 --> 00:01:37.150
earn widespread acclaim and accolades, but
Brody won the Academy Award for Best Actor,

00:01:37.150 --> 00:01:40.619
making him the youngest winner ever in that
category.

00:01:40.619 --> 00:01:42.260
Jim Carrey

00:01:42.260 --> 00:01:47.180
In 1999, Jim Carrey took a deep dive into
method acting when he played comedian Andy

00:01:47.180 --> 00:01:49.220
Kaufman in Man on the Moon.

00:01:49.220 --> 00:01:54.479
While a handful of stories from the set made
it into the news over the years, a 2017 Netflix

00:01:54.479 --> 00:01:59.350
documentary revealed many more jaw-dropping
moments from the film's production.

00:01:59.350 --> 00:02:03.820
In Jim &amp; Andy: The Great Beyond, it becomes
clear that Carrey's dedication to the role

00:02:03.820 --> 00:02:08.349
went way beyond spitting in the face of professional
wrestler Jerry Lawler.

00:02:08.349 --> 00:02:13.189
During the shoot, Carrey didn't just play
Kaufman, he channeled him, to the point that

00:02:13.189 --> 00:02:16.290
he would refer to "Jim Carrey" as a completely
different person.

00:02:16.290 --> 00:02:26.700
"Jim had trouble later on and...and it kind
of threw him for a loop and he got angry."

00:02:26.700 --> 00:02:33.029
As Andy, Carrey also had emotional reunions
with members of Kaufman's family, including

00:02:33.029 --> 00:02:37.110
an hour-long conversation with his daughter,
who never got a chance to meet her father

00:02:37.110 --> 00:02:40.059
before he passed away from lung cancer in
1984.

00:02:40.059 --> 00:02:50.800
"So that day she came into Andy's trailer
and talked to her father."

00:02:50.800 --> 00:02:55.739
As Kaufman's vulgar alter ego Tony Clifton,
Carrey went on drunken tirades, crashed a

00:02:55.739 --> 00:03:00.159
convertible, and stormed into Steven Spielberg's
office to chat with him about Jaws.

00:03:00.159 --> 00:03:04.849
"I have this uh gentleman in the lobby that
is looking for Steven Spielberg."

00:03:04.849 --> 00:03:06.980
"I would like to see the real shark.

00:03:06.980 --> 00:03:09.230
This shark is a phony shark."

00:03:09.230 --> 00:03:13.919
Even though Carrey won praise for his performance,
the role clearly took a toll on him, and he

00:03:13.919 --> 00:03:17.529
later claimed that he could barely remember
who he was after filming.

00:03:17.529 --> 00:03:19.559
"But wasn't it funny?

00:03:19.559 --> 00:03:23.049
Yes, it was funny, Andy.

00:03:23.049 --> 00:03:24.459
It was."

00:03:24.459 --> 00:03:25.949
Nicolas Cage

00:03:25.949 --> 00:03:30.750
He might be better known today for his goofy
action movies and eccentric spending habits,

00:03:30.750 --> 00:03:34.319
but Nicolas Cage is also dedicated to method
acting.

00:03:34.319 --> 00:03:38.839
His approach to acting has yielded mixed results
over the years; he earned acclaim for films

00:03:38.839 --> 00:03:43.819
like Adaptation and Leaving Las Vegas, and
derision for movies like The Wicker Man.

00:03:43.819 --> 00:03:45.999
"Aahhh my eyes!

00:03:45.999 --> 00:03:47.459
My eyes!

00:03:47.459 --> 00:03:48.459
Aaahhh!"

00:03:48.459 --> 00:03:52.659
One of his best method roles dates back to
the early days of his acting career, when

00:03:52.659 --> 00:03:56.389
he was cast in the 1984 war drama Birdy.

00:03:56.389 --> 00:04:01.540
In the film, Cage plays a disfigured Vietnam
veteran who visits a friend from the war in

00:04:01.540 --> 00:04:03.339
a psychiatric hospital.

00:04:03.339 --> 00:04:09.139
To prepare for the part, the then 19-year-old
Cage had his wisdom teeth extracted without

00:04:09.139 --> 00:04:10.139
anesthesia.

00:04:10.139 --> 00:04:15.269
He also spent weeks with his face bandaged
day and night, so he could experience the

00:04:15.269 --> 00:04:18.890
physical pain and discomfort his character
was going through.

00:04:18.890 --> 00:04:23.430
His impressive dedication to the part shows,
and Cage's work in Birdy remains one of his

00:04:23.430 --> 00:04:25.650
best early performances.

00:04:25.650 --> 00:04:27.100
Christian Bale

00:04:27.100 --> 00:04:31.790
While method acting began as a way to harness
personal experiences and emotions as a way

00:04:31.790 --> 00:04:37.200
to play a part more convincingly, in recent
years it's become almost synonymous with drastic

00:04:37.200 --> 00:04:39.640
physical transformations.

00:04:39.640 --> 00:04:44.090
In one prominent example, Christian Bale made
headlines and jeopardized his health when

00:04:44.090 --> 00:04:51.560
he lost a jaw-dropping 63 pounds to play the
insomnia-plagued Trevor Reznik in 2004's The

00:04:51.560 --> 00:04:52.560
Machinist.

00:04:52.560 --> 00:04:57.120
For weeks before filming began, Bale survived
on just an apple and a can of tuna fish a

00:04:57.120 --> 00:04:58.120
day.

00:04:58.120 --> 00:05:03.620
According to his Machinist costar Michael
Ironside, the drastic diet wasn't even necessary.

00:05:03.620 --> 00:05:07.530
The screenwriter for the movie had put some
example weights into the script based on his

00:05:07.530 --> 00:05:08.630
own height.

00:05:08.630 --> 00:05:13.330
When the much-taller Bale saw the figures,
he decided to go for those weights instead

00:05:13.330 --> 00:05:16.180
of something more realistic for his bigger
frame.

00:05:16.180 --> 00:05:21.650
After filming wrapped, Bale bulked up again,
gaining 109 pounds in just six months for

00:05:21.650 --> 00:05:24.090
his next role in Batman Begins.

00:05:24.090 --> 00:05:28.800
He's gone on to use drastic weight changes
in preparation for other parts, too; more

00:05:28.800 --> 00:05:33.780
recently, Bale shaved his head and packed
on the pounds to play former U.S. vice president

00:05:33.780 --> 00:05:38.010
Dick Cheney in the 2018 biopic Backseat.

00:05:38.010 --> 00:05:39.620
Martin Sheen

00:05:39.620 --> 00:05:44.760
After director Francis Ford Coppola fired
Harvey Keitel from 1979's Apocalypse Now two

00:05:44.760 --> 00:05:49.760
weeks into production, he picked Martin Sheen
to take Keitel's place as Captain Benjamin

00:05:49.760 --> 00:05:51.390
L. Willard.

00:05:51.390 --> 00:05:56.130
Sheen's approach was to use experiences from
his own life to bring himself closer to his

00:05:56.130 --> 00:05:57.130
character.

00:05:57.130 --> 00:05:59.930
Sheen certainly brought his own demons with
him to the set.

00:05:59.930 --> 00:06:05.190
His personal struggle with alcoholism played
a key role in the film's famous opening scene.

00:06:05.190 --> 00:06:10.200
The actor was completely drunk that day, and
Coppola captured the unscripted mirror-smashing,

00:06:10.200 --> 00:06:13.400
weeping, and blood-smearing that followed.

00:06:13.400 --> 00:06:16.940
When production moved to the jungle, Sheen
told his friends that he wasn't sure he'd

00:06:16.940 --> 00:06:20.190
make it out of the shoot alive, and he almost
didn't.

00:06:20.190 --> 00:06:24.890
Sheen actually suffered a heart attack and
crawled for a quarter mile to a road, where

00:06:24.890 --> 00:06:27.310
he was found and airlifted to a hospital.

00:06:27.310 --> 00:06:30.030
He was back on the set just six weeks later.

00:06:30.030 --> 00:06:34.610
In this case, at least, it wasn't just one
actor who was uncontrollable on set.

00:06:34.610 --> 00:06:39.500
From the tropical diseases that ravaged the
crew to actual cadavers showing up on the

00:06:39.500 --> 00:06:41.620
set, the entire shoot was chaotic.

00:06:41.620 --> 00:06:46.580
If anything, it would have been weird if Martin
Sheen hadn't acted weird.

00:06:46.580 --> 00:06:47.780
Matt Damon

00:06:47.780 --> 00:06:52.270
He may be famous today for movies like Good
Will Hunting and the Jason Bourne franchise,

00:06:52.270 --> 00:06:57.410
but in the early 1990s, Matt Damon was just
another unknown actor.

00:06:57.410 --> 00:07:02.410
All that changed when he was cast in the 1996
war drama Courage Under Fire.

00:07:02.410 --> 00:07:06.820
To prepare for the role of an ex-medic with
a drug addiction, Damon dropped down to a

00:07:06.820 --> 00:07:13.720
mere 139 pounds by running 13 miles a day
and subsisting on a diet made almost entirely

00:07:13.720 --> 00:07:15.210
of chicken breast.

00:07:15.210 --> 00:07:19.840
The drastic weight loss took an enormous toll
on his body, after filming wrapped, he had

00:07:19.840 --> 00:07:23.760
to take special medication for a year and
a half just to repair the damage done to his

00:07:23.760 --> 00:07:25.640
adrenal gland.

00:07:25.640 --> 00:07:27.330
Daniel Day-Lewis

00:07:27.330 --> 00:07:32.260
For much of his career, three-time Oscar winner
Daniel Day-Lewis has been notorious for his

00:07:32.260 --> 00:07:34.670
intense approach to method acting.

00:07:34.670 --> 00:07:39.110
The actor's behavior on and off the set is
the stuff of legend.

00:07:39.110 --> 00:07:44.550
In preparation for his role as John Proctor
in The Crucible, Day-Lewis went back to basics,

00:07:44.550 --> 00:07:45.550
literally.

00:07:45.550 --> 00:07:51.580
He lived for months on the set of the 17th-century
village with no electricity or running water,

00:07:51.580 --> 00:07:56.270
built his character's home by hand with only
tools from that era, and even gave up his

00:07:56.270 --> 00:07:59.620
beloved motorcycle in favor of a horse.

00:07:59.620 --> 00:08:01.490
Ashton Kutcher

00:08:01.490 --> 00:08:07.320
After Apple CEO Steve Jobs passed away in
2011, the race was on in Hollywood to develop

00:08:07.320 --> 00:08:10.270
a feature film based on the mogul's remarkable
life.

00:08:10.270 --> 00:08:16.060
For the 2013 independent film Jobs, director
Joshua Michael Stern picked Ashton Kutcher

00:08:16.060 --> 00:08:21.100
to portray the complex computer pioneer, and
the actor spent three months preparing for

00:08:21.100 --> 00:08:26.780
the role, watching hundreds of hours of archival
footage to understand Jobs' accent, posture,

00:08:26.780 --> 00:08:28.460
and facial expressions.

00:08:28.460 --> 00:08:34.319
He didn't stop there, Kutcher also tried adopting
a fruit-only diet, similar to the one Jobs

00:08:34.319 --> 00:08:38.349
adopted as part of an alternative medicine
regimen to treat his cancer.

00:08:38.349 --> 00:08:44.329
For Kutcher, the drastic diet caused its own
health issues, two days before filming began,

00:08:44.329 --> 00:08:47.420
he ended up in the hospital with pancreas
problems.

00:08:47.420 --> 00:08:50.520
Kutcher explained: "I was like doubled over
in pain.

00:08:50.520 --> 00:08:53.189
My pancreas levels were completely out of
whack.

00:08:53.189 --> 00:08:55.029
It was really terrifying."

00:08:55.029 --> 00:08:59.980
In the end, Kutcher's dedication paid off,
while Jobs received mixed reviews and was

00:08:59.980 --> 00:09:04.230
criticized for its historical inaccuracies,
Kutcher's performance earned praise.

00:09:04.230 --> 00:09:06.160
"What are you...you gonna fire me?"

00:09:06.160 --> 00:09:07.160
"No!

00:09:07.160 --> 00:09:09.240
I already fired you!"

00:09:10.240 --> 00:09:12.500
"Why are you still here?"

